Both verified byte-identical to the published copies on 2026-09-09, fetched
from https://ekr.financnasprava.sk/Formulare/XSD/<file>

TWO VZORY, and the split is real. FS SR keeps one XSD per vzor and keeps the
old ones, so a period is filed in the structure in force AT THE TIME — unlike
the Czech side, which overwrites one file in place and files every period in
the current structure.

  svdph2010.xsd   1. 1. 2010 – 31. 12. 2019
  svdph20.xsd     1. 1. 2020 – open

What separates them is the Quick Fixes (smernica 2018/1910, § 8a zákona
o DPH) from 1. 1. 2020: the SV gains a call-off-stock register
(`zaznamCast2`), and the page goes from 27 records to 12 + 12. The 2020 vzor
also drops `oznacenie` and `pocet2Stran`, and replaces the structured fax with
an email address. Same root (`dokument`) in both, so the root element cannot
tell you which one you are holding.

NEITHER FILE CAN PROVE IT IS CURRENT. `svdph2010.xsd` stamps itself only
"Suhrnny vykaz (dan z pridanej hodnoty)" — no vintage, no revision.
`svdph20.xsd` adds "2020", a vintage and not a revision. A file revised in
place therefore validates exactly as before and says nothing about it, which
is why the digests are pinned here rather than trusted from the annotation.

REFRESH PROCEDURE
  1. Fetch both names above and compare the md5 with the lines here.
  2. Check the STATUS CODE, not the size: a miss returns 404 with a
     ~1 245-byte HTML error page, which is a perfectly plausible file size.
  3. Probe for a newer vintage as well as a _v2 suffix on an existing name —
     FS SR published a DPPO revision as dppo2025_v2.xsd beside an unchanged
     dppo2025.xsd, and a digest comparison on a pinned name cannot see that.
